schema: test/v2
level: working-unit
epic: E-EXPORT
wu: E-EXPORT.W3
branch: wu/export/har-in
tests:
  unit:
    count: 44
    covers: "src/api/har.rs (28 + 3 proptests) - the entry-to-flow mapping for a full exchange, with the host re-derived rather than read; the captured instant preserved while the id is minted fresh; entries inserted oldest-first so a newest-first ring reads the file in file order (the export writes newest-first, so preserving file order would land a capture backwards); an unmeasured duration recovered as None from -1, from a negative, and from an absent time; the timings phases never read back as the duration (they are zeroed beside a -1 total, so re-deriving from them would contradict the total); a fractional time rounded rather than truncated; header lists keeping order, duplicates and empty values, and a valueless header accepted because the wire allows one; an entry with no response becoming a request-only flow (the export never writes one, but a foreign HAR may carry one and W7's curl import produces exactly this shape); a missing or empty httpVersion falling back to the wire default; the nine derived and unretained fields (queryString, both cookie lists, statusText, redirectURL, timings, headersSize, cache, and unknown members like serverIPAddress) read past in silence, each because it was either derived from a header the flow still carries or was never Trawler's to hold; text and base64 bodies decoded to their exact bytes; the bytes outranking a size that disagrees with them, asserted through retained_bytes because the ring is weighted by what it holds and letting a client's arithmetic set that weight would put eviction under the file's control; all four marked body states restored from the reason tokens the projection wrote; a streamed body keeping the content type it was streaming; a too-large body keeping the size that got it dropped; a too_large marker that lost its size degrading to ReadError rather than inventing one, since the size is the only thing separating the two; the two foreign-HAR rows where a body with no text but a size claiming one existed becomes an explicit not-captured rather than Empty (calling it empty would assert a 4MB response was a 204 - the exact loss BodyRec exists to prevent); a foreign tool's comment tested in three positions, restoring neither streaming nor too_large and never overriding a measured zero; a missing content object as Empty; both request-leg asymmetries, where an absent postData is read through the bodySize beside it (0 or absent is Empty, because a GET with no body is the common case; anything else is not-captured) because HAR's postData has no shape for an uncaptured body; and every refusal from the contract, each asserting the reason names its field, plus eleven malformations in one table and an entry-level fault naming its index so a caller with a 900-entry file can locate it. Proptests: a_flow_survives_export_and_reimport is the WU's central claim and an all-inputs one - over generated flows (three methods, four URI shapes including an unparseable one, all five body variants on both legs, four content types, header sets with duplicates and empty values, present and absent responses, measured and unmeasured durations) an exported and re-imported flow compares equal on exactly what DEC-30 says the format preserves, and its body helper asserts the BOUNDARY of what it does not: only the request leg may collapse an uncaptured state, and never a captured or empty body, so the collapse HAR's postData shape forces cannot silently widen. Mutation-checked: removing the entry reversal and removing the too-large size restoration each turn the property red. the_parser_never_panics_on_arbitrary_bytes and the_parser_never_panics_on_a_near_miss_document cover the fuzz-shaped half of the epic's expected result 7 that no list of named malformations can; the second is the load-bearing one, since random bytes mostly fail at the JSON gate, so its generator builds HAR-shaped documents whose every member may be the wrong type, out of range or missing. src/flow.rs (1) - Flow::new still yields Captured so the capture path and the recorder never learn a new field exists (asserted rather than assumed, because a silent regression there would mismark every live flow). src/api/view.rs (2) - the provenance mark on both projections, since a client that filters on a field it cannot see in the rows it gets back has to re-fetch to confirm; and the token round trip, asserting the listing filter parses back exactly what the projections render so a token cannot be spelled one way out and another way in. That round-trip test moved here from flow.rs with the vocabulary it tests, per the review. src/store.rs (7) - a batch landing in the order given, ahead of a pre-existing captured flow; the count returned and the byte weight of what it holds; eviction under the cap like any insert; one RequestCaptured per flow with ResponseCaptured only where the entry had a response, and the imported duration carried onto that event; the source filter alone and composed with method; and the cap accessor. The pre-existing ring_accounting proptest was EXTENDED to interleave import batches between inserts rather than duplicated beside it, so the byte invariant now covers the new entry path. src/api/routes.rs (5) - the count response and the listing that follows it; a refusal saying why and storing nothing, over three malformation classes; the source filter reaching the store on the listing and on the export; and an unrecognised value refused by name on both, because a silently empty listing reads to a caller exactly like nothing matched. src/api/mod.rs (1) - import_is_a_post_route_of_its_own asserts the static /flows/import segment resolves and answers only POST (a GET is 405, not a flow-id lookup), and the pre-existing preflight test now also asserts the grant advertises GET, POST, OPTIONS, because a webview's fetch cannot POST to an origin whose grant omits the method."
  integration:
    count: 7
    covers: "tests/api.rs over the real axum router, each seeding a four-flow fixture that covers every body state the format has to carry (a text response with a JSON request body, a binary request body with a 204, a streamed response, a too-large response). an_exported_capture_reimports_as_the_same_flows_marked_imported is the WU requirement at the seam: it exports a seeded capture, imports the bytes into a fresh store, asserts the count, asserts the listing order survived, asserts every re-imported row carries the mark while the capture it came from does not, then compares eleven fields (method, uri, status, both HTTP versions, started_at, duration_ms, both header lists, both body objects) on the POST exchange and asserts the id differs because an id is this store's handle rather than the file's - the epic's expected result 2. the_source_filter_selects_each_kind_across_listing_and_export imports into a non-empty capture and asserts the total grew rather than being replaced, that each source value selects exactly its four rows, that the export inherits the same filter and selects the same URLs (so /flows/export?source=imported works with no export change), that source composes with method, and that an unrecognised value is a 400 naming the parameter. an_imported_flow_is_an_ordinary_flow proves UC-6's claim through the endpoints a client actually uses: GET /flows/{id} shows the mark and the URI, the raw request body endpoint returns the exact bytes, and the response body endpoint with ?decode=1 returns pretty-printed JSON - the decode surface working on a body that arrived from a file. an_imported_flow_appears_on_the_stream_as_it_lands subscribes to /stream first, then imports, and asserts the SSE frame carries the request event and the imported URI. an_uncaptured_body_survives_the_round_trip_as_a_client_sees_it asserts the streamed body is streaming again, the too-large one is not_captured, a real 204 stays empty, and the too-large body's detail view still reports reason too_large with actual 9000000 - the honest-body contract inverted, at the client boundary. a_refused_import_leaves_the_store_untouched drives eight malformations (not JSON, no log, version 1.3, no log.entries, an entry-level fault, a bad timestamp, an impossible status, invalid base64) and after each asserts the listing is byte-identical to what it was before, then asserts an empty entry list is a 200 reporting zero rather than an error - the epic's expected result 7. the_same_file_imported_twice_yields_two_independent_sets asserts each import adds one, that the two imported rows carry different ids and the same original instant, and that the pre-existing captured flow was neither touched nor marked - the epic plan's questions 3 and 4 as assertions. One pre-existing test was edited rather than added: W1's the_unparameterised_listing_keeps_its_pre_paging_shape pins the summary row's exact field set and went red on source, which is the lock doing its job; its expected set gained the field and its purpose is unchanged. No new snapshot: W2's the_har_document_shape_is_locked is unchanged because this WU adds no field to the exported document, and the two new wire contracts are the source field (already locked by that field-set test, which is exactly why it went red) and the single-key import response, fully asserted above - a second snapshot of {imported: n} would lock nothing the assertions do not. Stated as a deliberate call rather than an omission."
  e2e:
    waived: "The import's e2e is the epic's own tests/e2e_export.rs steps 5 and 6 (POST /flows/import with the exported bytes, asserting the listing returns the same flows each source=imported, that ?source=captured is empty, and that each is readable at /flows/{id} and its body endpoints; then a truncated copy refused with the store still holding exactly the previous import), which lands with the epic per its e2e plan. A second e2e here would boot a second engine to drive the same seam through the same transport. The regression evidence that this WU left the capture path untouched - it touches that path only by adding a field Flow::new fills - is that both existing e2e suites (e2e_capture, e2e_h2) run green unchanged and no pre-existing assertion was altered except W1's summary field-set lock, which is the intended contract addition. The seam was additionally hand-run against the running system: a temporary harness (deleted before the commit) booted serve(Config) on ephemeral ports, because the default 7420/7421 were held by another session's process and killing it was not this WU's call. Real proxied traffic through the proxy to a local origin, then GET /flows/export returned 774 bytes; posting them back returned {\"imported\":1}; the listing showed the imported row carrying source imported beside the captured one at the same started_at; ?source=imported returned exactly the one row; a truncated copy was refused 400 'not a JSON document: EOF while parsing an object at line 1 column 387' with the store still holding 2 flows; ?source=nope was 400 'source must be captured or imported'; and the preflight advertised GET, POST, OPTIONS. The exported time was a genuine measured 0 on a sub-millisecond loopback exchange and round-tripped as 0, not confused with the unmeasured -1 case that the unit tests cover separately."
note: "E-EXPORT.W3 delivers POST /flows/import: a HAR 1.2 document read back as flows marked imported, and the source field it forces onto the domain model. Three things carry the WU. First, the parser lives in src/api/har.rs beside the projection it inverts, which is DEC-30's own stated consequence - a second module could drift into a format Trawler writes but cannot read, and the two directions share the reason tokens, the version constant and the timestamp format. The deserializable types are separate types in that module, because what Trawler writes is a fixed shape and what it reads is whatever a conforming tool emitted; one struct serving both would make the export lax or the import strict. Second, the whole document parses into a Vec<Flow> before Store::import is called at all, so 'never a partially-populated store' is structural rather than a cleanup path: a refused document leaves nothing to roll back because nothing was inserted, and one bad entry refuses the document naming its index because a caller who cannot tell which of 900 entries landed cannot recover. Third, provenance is one closed-enum field in the same ring rather than a second collection: an imported flow is read by the same detail and body endpoints, announced on the same events, and evicted by the same byte rule, and Store::import takes one lock for the whole batch so a concurrent capture cannot interleave into the middle of an imported one. Store::import announces RequestCaptured per flow plus ResponseCaptured where the entry had a response, reusing the existing events because a subscriber's job is 'a flow appeared and here it is', which is true of an import; W4's clear is where a genuinely new variant is warranted, because removal is a fact no existing event carries. Three differences from what the plan assumed, none a change of contract. The refusal is plain text, not a JSON {error} envelope: the plan asserted the JSON shape was already W1's convention and it is not - bad_request has returned plain text since before this epic - so the plan was wrong about the existing code rather than choosing a new shape, and following the real convention keeps one refusal shape across the surface instead of making ?source='s refusal differ from ?limit='s on the same endpoint. The CORS widening landed here rather than in W4: the epic plan scheduled it with DELETE assuming that would be the first mutating method, and import is, so the grant became GET, POST, OPTIONS now and W4 adds DELETE to the same list - placement, not reasoning, and DEC-31 records why it extends DEC-12 (still loopback-bound, still nothing a local curl cannot reach) rather than reversing it. And architecture.html was edited, where W2's result predicted W4 would move that line: an export adds a read, but an import adds an ingest, so the seam diagram's 'the control API is purely a subscriber and reader - it never mutates capture state' and the invariant row's 'read-only API bound to loopback' both went false and were narrowed to what is true; it is rule-bearing T1, so that commit carries Reviewed-By: key vuluu via mr commit. Five smaller calls the plan left to the build are recorded in the result: the fallible into_filter replacing From (a source value can be invalid, which no previous filter field could be, and From cannot fail without pushing the parse into the store layer or silently falling back to no filter - the empty-listing failure the plan forbade); the imported flow's SSE duration as unwrap_or(0), because the event's field is not optional and widening it would touch every subscriber for a fact the listing already serves as null; a fractional HAR time rounded and clamped rather than truncated or wrapped; a foreign comment beside a measured zero staying Empty, since the marker is what carries state and a comment that is not the marker carries nothing (the first cut keyed the empty case on comment.is_none(), which turned a genuine 204 with foreign prose into a not-captured; caught by a test that had asserted the wrong expectation, then corrected in both); and postData.mimeType deliberately unread, because the request's content type lives in its preserved Content-Type header and postData only ever restated it. cargo test green (154 lib, 27 api, 3 ca, 6 e2e_capture, 4 e2e_h2), up from 110 lib and 20 api; clippy pedantic clean with --all-targets --all-features; fmt clean. No new dependency, and no new proptest regression seed - the three new properties found no failure. No UI-rendering file in the diff." The code review ran five isolated finders over the WU diff with adversarial verification of every candidate: ten candidates, seven refuted, three real (one important, two nits), all three fixed rather than waived. The important one was altitude: FlowSource::as_str/parse put the wire tokens on the domain enum, and the verifier established the decisive fact - nothing below the API boundary needs the string form, because FlowFilter holds the enum and store.rs compares enum values, so both functions' only non-test callers were in src/api/. W2's review had refuted the mirror-image proposal (moving the body reason tokens down onto NotCapturedReason) on exactly those grounds, and architecture.html homes wire shape in the API package. Fixed the way W2 established for rfc3339: both directions now live in view.rs as source_token/parse_source, one home for the vocabulary, with the round-trip test moved beside them. The second real finding was efficiency: Store::import cloned each flow's request and response inside the ring mutex while insert and set_response both clone before taking it, and the proxy's capture path contends on that same lock, so P2 applies to who is stalled rather than who holds. Now cloned before the lock. The measurement corrected the finding's own framing - a verifier's synthetic benchmark reported a ~17x reduction in total hold, but measuring what a real capture actually waits gives ~44ms versus ~49ms on a 2048-flow batch, about 5ms, because a contending capture queues behind the flows still ahead of it rather than the whole batch. This fix deliberately ships WITHOUT a regression test: three were attempted (a lock-held-from-another-thread timing assertion, a concurrent-capture contention test, and a direct hold measurement) and all three passed against the unfixed code, because at any batch size a unit test can afford the two shapes are indistinguishable. A test that cannot fail is worse than no test since it falsely implies a guard, so the fix is kept on structural grounds (it matches insert and set_response) and the absence of a guard is recorded in the result rather than papered over. The third was a simplification nit: response_body's fallthrough was two sequential decisions sharing a reason variable, the first with no else, and is now one match over (marker, size); the verifier proved the single match compiles clippy-clean under this repo's pedantic settings, refuting the concern that it would reintroduce the match_same_arms warning an earlier iteration hit. Seven candidates were refuted, and in refuting them the finders independently confirmed the WU's load-bearing claims: the parse-before-store invariant is structurally enforced, the oldest-first reversal is genuinely exercised rather than tautologically validated, bytes outrank a declared size, no parsing path can panic, reason_token reads back exactly what not_captured_comment writes, HAR_VERSION and the timestamp format have one definition each, and Store::import shares Ring::evict and announce_evictions with insert. Notable refutations: import returning the accepted count rather than the post-eviction survivor count (UC-6 commits explicitly to "how many entries it took", DEC-31 accepts eviction as a designed cost, and a survivor count would be stale the moment a concurrent capture lands); a SystemTime::from panic path the fuzz generator cannot reach (empirically unreachable, since time 0.3 without large-dates caps years at plus/minus 9999 and the extremes were run without a panic); a params-only postData reading as not-captured (exactly what DEC-30's sentinel-not-invention rule requires, since reconstructing wire bytes from parsed params would fabricate bytes never on the wire); seeded_store duplicating W2's har_fixture_store (extending that helper would churn the insta snapshot it feeds); extracting a shared Ring::push (the rubric's explicit "not its job: inventing new abstractions to force sharing"); decode_body's avoidable copy (the rubric's excluded micro-optimization outside hot paths); and a claim that the plan had specified view.rs for the tokens so the placement was an unrecorded deviation - the plan never mentions the conversion's location at all, so shipping it on the enum resolved an open question rather than deviating, and the result's what-shipped table recorded it.
